{"name":"Sweet Porridge","hex":"#d5caaa","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tasting of sugars.","Retaining a portion of sugar."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used as a positive response to good news or information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweet"},{"term":"porridge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dish made of grain or legumes, milk or water, heated and stirred until thick and typically eaten for breakfast.","Oat porridge; oatmeal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/porridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweet Porridge","blocks":[{"text":"\"Sweet Porridge\", often known in English under the title of \"The Magic Porridge Pot\", is a folkloric German fairy tale recorded by the Brothers Grimm, as tale number 103 in Grimm's Fairy Tales, in the 19th century. It is Aarne–Thompson–Uther type 565, \"the magic mill\". Other tales of this type include Why the Sea Is Salt and The Water Mother."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Otto_Ubbelohde_-_Der_s%C3%BC%C3%9Fe_Brei.jpg/330px-Otto_Ubbelohde_-_Der_s%C3%BC%C3%9Fe_Brei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_Porridge","wikidata":"Q471233"},{"title":"Porridge","blocks":[{"text":"Porridge is a type of semi-solid food made by soaking, poaching or boiling, in milk or water, ground, crushed or chopped starchy plants, typically grain. It is a traditional staple food of many countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Oatmeal_%281%29.jpg/330px-Oatmeal_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porridge","wikidata":"Q186817"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Otto_Ubbelohde_-_Der_s%C3%BC%C3%9Fe_Brei.jpg/500px-Otto_Ubbelohde_-_Der_s%C3